        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Marxism–Leninism</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 1924 CE – present</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Thinkers</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-lenin">Vladimir Lenin</a>, whose Bolshevism supplied its method of revolution; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Joseph Stalin</a>, who defined it and made it the Soviet orthodoxy; Karl Radek, who proposed the name Marxism–Leninism–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stalinism">Stalinism</a> in 1934; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leon-trotsky">Leon Trotsky</a>, its leading critic from the left within the party; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josip-broz-tito">Josip Broz Tito</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enver-hoxha">Enver Hoxha</a>, who produced rival variants; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rosa-luxemburg">Rosa Luxemburg</a>, an early critic of the vanguard</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Key texts</span><span class="info-stat-val">Lenin's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperialism-the-highest-stage-of-capitalism">Imperialism, the Highest Stage of Capitalism</a> (1917); Stalin's Concerning Questions of Leninism (1926), Dialectical and Historical Materialism and the textbook History of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-party-of-the-soviet-union">Communist Party of the Soviet Union</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a>) (1938); Khrushchev's On the Cult of Personality and Its Consequences (25 February 1956)</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Core ideas</span><span class="info-stat-val">a two-stage revolution, socialism before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a>; a vanguard party organised through democratic centralism; a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-party-state">one-party state</a>; a planned economy with the means of production under state control; socialism in one country; dialectical and historical materialism; atheism; opposition to colonialism and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperialism">imperialism</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Today</span><span class="info-stat-val">the official ideology of the ruling parties of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, and of Hoxhaist, Maoist and neo-Stalinist parties elsewhere; the state ideology of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-korea">North Korea</a> is derived from it; Maoist insurgencies continue in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a></span></div>
