    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Marxism–Leninism held that a vanguard party, organised through democratic centralism, would seize power for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/proletariat">proletariat</a> and build a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-party-state">one-party state</a> with a planned economy as the road to a classless, stateless society.</li><li>It was formulated after Lenin's death in 1924 by Stalin and his supporters, who set socialism in one country against Trotsky's permanent revolution, and it was the state ideology of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> until the dissolution of 1991.</li><li>Its record in power is disputed: it brought rapid industrialisation, mass education and welfare, and also purges, labour camps and famines, and scholars disagree on how far the ideology itself explains the repression.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In 1934 Karl Radek proposed the name Marxism–Leninism–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stalinism">Stalinism</a> in Pravda, but the name did not catch on, since Stalin and the party's ideologists preferred to keep Marxism–Leninism.</p></aside>
    </section>

