    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1917</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> seize power in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/october-revolution">October Revolution</a> and form the Russian Soviet Federative Socialist Republic</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1926</span><span class="rf-text">Stalin's Concerning Questions of Leninism presents Marxism–Leninism as a separate communist ideology</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1928</span><span class="rf-text">The first five-year plan begins the rapid industrialisation and collectivisation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1936</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-purge">Great Purge</a> begins, lasting until 1938; the Soviet Constitution of the same year is adopted</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1949</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a> establishes the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a> on 1 October after the Communist victory in the civil war</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1956</span><span class="rf-text">Khrushchev denounces Stalin's cult of personality at the 20th Congress of the Soviet party</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1989</span><span class="rf-text">Revolts across Central and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a> bring down the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-bloc">Eastern Bloc</a> governments, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s government ends protests by force at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiananmen">Tiananmen</a> Square</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1991</span><span class="rf-text">Gorbachev announces the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dissolution-of-the-soviet-union">dissolution of the Soviet Union</a> on 25 December</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/nicholas-ii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nicholas-ii.jpg" alt="Nicholas II" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/nicholas-ii">Nicholas II</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mikola_II_(cropped)-2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author (originally uploaded to Wikimedia Commons on 22 August 2012 by Peruanec), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Marxism–Leninism grew from the practice of the Bolshevik faction of the Russian Social Democratic Labour Party. Lenin made the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> a vanguard of professional revolutionaries who practised democratic centralism, and he drew from the revolution of 1905 the conclusion that a small, disciplined party could sponsor a socialist revolution through agitation and propaganda. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperialism-the-highest-stage-of-capitalism">Imperialism, the Highest Stage of Capitalism</a> (1917) he argued that capitalist expansion led to colonial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperialism">imperialism</a> and to wars such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">Great War</a>. The Bolsheviks seized power in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/october-revolution">October Revolution</a> of 1917, after the abdication of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicholas-ii">Nicholas II</a>, and fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-civil-war">Russian Civil War</a> of 1917 to 1922.</p><p>The name and the doctrine came afterwards. After Lenin's death on 21 January 1924, Stalin, Kamenev and Zinoviev formed a troika against Trotsky, and the party chose Stalin's policy of socialism in one country over Trotsky's permanent revolution in 1925 and endorsed it in 1927. Stalin's Concerning Questions of Leninism (1926) represented Marxism–Leninism as a separate ideology with a global hierarchy of communist parties. Stalin's Dialectical and Historical Materialism became its foundation philosophy, and his 1938 textbook popularised it.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="ideas">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Core Ideas</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/karl-marx.jpg" alt="Karl Marx" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Karl Marx</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karl_Marx_001_restored.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">John Jabez Edwin Mayall, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Marxism–Leninism holds that a two-stage communist revolution is needed to replace <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">capitalism</a>. A vanguard party, organised through democratic centralism, seizes power on behalf of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/proletariat">proletariat</a> and founds a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-party-state">one-party state</a>, which controls the means of production and suppresses the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bourgeoisie">bourgeoisie</a> and counter-revolution, in order to pave the way for a classless and stateless communist society. Its adherents treated class struggle as the dominating principle of social change and advocated a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> of the proletariat. In the economy, scientific planning replaces market and price mechanisms, wages vary with skill and the intensity of work, and personal property that does not involve mass production stays outside state control.</p><p>It supports universal welfare, free education and health care, and the emancipation of women through secular marriage, easier divorce and the entry of women into industrial work. It aims at what its writers called the New Soviet man, a cultured citizen with proletarian class consciousness, and it is atheist, drawing on Hegel, Feuerbach, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Marx</a> and Lenin. In international relations it opposes colonialism and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperialism">imperialism</a> and supports anti-fascist popular fronts. Rachel Walker calls the term an empty one that is open to redefinition by each ruling party.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="spread">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Spread &amp; Variants</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/josip-broz-tito"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/josip-broz-tito.jpg" alt="Josip Broz Tito" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/josip-broz-tito">Josip Broz Tito</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Josip_Broz_Tito_uniform_portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Stalin's rise brought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-international">Communist International</a> under one orthodoxy by the late 1920s, and Marxist–Leninist parties led much of the armed resistance in the countries occupied by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis powers</a>; in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> the Partisans under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josip-broz-tito">Josip Broz Tito</a> liberated territory and founded the communist state. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> installed Marxist–Leninist governments in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a> it had cleared of the Axis, and in 1949 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a> founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a>. Communists also took power in North <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, in the 1970s in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> and a reunited Vietnam, and in African states including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-yemen">South Yemen</a> was the only openly communist state in the Arab world.</p><p>Variants split the movement. Stalin expelled Tito's Communist Party of Yugoslavia from the Cominform in 1948. After Khrushchev's denunciation of Stalin in 1956, Mao developed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maoism">Maoism</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sino-soviet-split">Sino-Soviet split</a> followed; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enver-hoxha">Enver Hoxha</a> then broke with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> over its rapprochement with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and theorised Hoxhaism. Guevarism, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh">Ho Chi Minh</a> Thought and Juche, which replaced Marxism–Leninism in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-korea">North Korea</a>'s constitution in 1992 and 2009, are other adaptations. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shining-path">Shining Path</a> developed Marxism–Leninism–Maoism.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="power">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">In Power</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/pol-pot"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pol-pot.jpg" alt="Pol Pot" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/pol-pot">Pol Pot</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pol_Pot_in_1978.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Editura Politică, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> the five-year plans from 1928 brought rapid industrialisation and urbanisation, and collectivisation reached 98.0% of agriculture by 1941. The same rapid industrialisation also resulted in the Soviet famine of 1930 to 1933, which killed millions. Stalin dismantled party democracy and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-purge">Great Purge</a> of 1936 to 1938 had the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nkvd">NKVD</a> arrest 1.5 million people in 1937 and 1938, of whom 681,692 were killed as enemies of the state. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulag">Gulag</a> held criminals, political dissidents, artists, intellectuals, homosexual people and religious opponents, and organised religion was repressed.</p><p>Elsewhere Marxist–Leninist rule had comparable features. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-leap-forward">Great Leap Forward</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> caused an estimated 15 to 55 million deaths between 1959 and 1961, mostly from starvation. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-rouge">Khmer Rouge</a>, a Maoist movement under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pol-pot">Pol Pot</a>, abolished money and private property and emptied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phnom-penh">Phnom Penh</a>, and its Killing Fields caused the deaths of 2,700,000 people by mass murder and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derg">Derg</a>'s imposition of its radical ideas led to civil war. The Soviet Union intervened in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> in 1979 to establish a communist state. Defenders such as Michael Parenti stress economic, social and cultural rights, and Michael Ellman and Sen noted gains in health and life expectancy.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="critiques">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Critiques &amp; Debates</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/amartya-sen"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amartya-sen.jpg" alt="Amartya Sen" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/amartya-sen">Amartya Sen</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amartya_Sen_20071128_cologne_cropped.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Elke Wetzig (Elya); derivative work by ChaudhryAzan, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Critics from across the spectrum have attacked Marxism–Leninism, mainly in its Stalinist and Maoist forms. Daniel Gray, Silvio Pons and David Martin Walker hold that killings and repression, such as the Red Terror, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-purge">Great Purge</a> and the Campaign to Suppress Counterrevolutionaries in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, came partly from the ideology, and Robert Service holds that atheism motivated mass religious persecution. Eric Voegelin called it inherently oppressive. Others reject such a link: Robert Vincent Daniels held that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marxism">Marxism</a> only justified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stalinism">Stalinism</a>, Graeme Gill called Stalinism a sharp break from earlier developments, and Michael Geyer and Sheila Fitzpatrick criticised the totalitarian model for obscuring how the systems worked. Scholars disagree over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holodomor">Holodomor</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amartya-sen">Amartya Sen</a> blamed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-chinese-famine">Great Chinese Famine</a> on a lack of democracy.</p><p>From the left, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rosa-luxemburg">Rosa Luxemburg</a> predicted that banning multi-party democracy would turn the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/proletariat">proletariat</a> into the dictatorship of a faction and then of an individual. Trotskyists described a degenerated workers' state under an unaccountable bureaucracy, and Raya Dunayevskaya and others called it state <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitalism">capitalism</a>. Historiography is polarised too: Stéphane Courtois, Steven Rosefielde and Rudolph Rummel wrote of mass deaths as a direct consequence of the ideology, while Noam Chomsky, Kristen Ghodsee, Seumas Milne and Michael Parenti charged them with exaggeration.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-gorbachev"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mikhail-gorbachev.jpg" alt="Mikhail Gorbachev" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-gorbachev">Mikhail Gorbachev</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:RIAN_archive_850809_General_Secretary_of_the_CPSU_CC_M._Gorbachev_(crop).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Vladimir Vyatkin / Владимир Вяткин; derivative work by Jbarta, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Khrushchev's speech of 25 February 1956 began <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/de-stalinization">de-Stalinisation</a>, the dismantling of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulag">Gulag</a> and the release of prisoners. In the 1980s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mikhail-gorbachev">Mikhail Gorbachev</a>'s perestroika and glasnost aimed at a return to a supposed ideal communist state, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solidarity-polish-trade-union">Solidarity</a> union in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> in 1980 had already shown resistance, and in 1989 revolts brought down the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-bloc">Eastern Bloc</a> governments; Ceaușescu was overthrown and executed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> dissolved in 1992, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> ended when Gorbachev announced its dissolution on 25 December 1991.</p><p>Only a few parties kept power afterwards: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> hold Marxism–Leninism as their official ideology, although they interpret it differently. China under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deng-xiaoping">Deng Xiaoping</a> reformed its economy while keeping one-party rule, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xi-jinping">Xi Jinping</a> has announced a deepening commitment to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marxism">Marxism</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> the Communist Party of the Russian Federation won the 1995 legislative election. Parties such as AKEL in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, the Portuguese Communist Party and the Communist Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> (Marxist) have taken part in governments or coalitions, while Maoist insurgencies continue in India, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>. Many historians and socialists dispute how faithful these movements were to the work of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Karl Marx</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Marxism–Leninism is the communist ideology formulated under Stalin after Lenin's death, built on a vanguard party, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-party-state">one-party state</a> and a planned economy. It was the state ideology of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and many other states, whose record of industrialisation, purges and famines is still disputed.</em></p>

